# Electron architecture

Apply the [repository working rules](../../AGENTS.md) and
[architecture map](../../README.ai). Desktop is a client of the selected
Nautilo server; Workbench is served by that server.

## Pairing and authentication

Keep the active server, Human session, tokens, workspace selection, grants,
and pending connection attempts scoped to their owning pairing. A late result
from a previous connection must not update its replacement. Read the existing
connection controllers and tests before changing switching or reconnect behavior.

Desktop sign-in uses the selected server's Logto configuration and the PKCE
flow under `apps/desktop/electron/auth/`. The auth window has no privileged
preload. Preserve navigation containment, callback/state validation, and token
storage boundaries. Authentication, PIN/biometric consent, and encryption
admission are separate checks; one must not substitute for another.

## Execution ownership

Electron owns Human/session/grant/policy/native authority. Relay Host owns
the authenticated server WebSocket through `RelayClient`. Computer Use Host
owns Cua supervision and provider parsing; Electron brokers admitted requests
using attested paths and private inherited pipes. Preserve cancellation,
generation fencing, and recovery without replaying ambiguous mutations.

Read [Relay/Host ownership](../../docs/relay-host-ownership.md) before changing
local execution. Browser, clipboard, filesystem, media, and credential handlers
must retain their existing sender and grant checks.

## Packaging and verification

The production app packages its client UI, preloads, native integrations, and
managed host/runtime artifacts. It does not package or start the Nautilo server,
Postgres, database migrations, or Compose infrastructure. Server lifecycle belongs
to the operator. Use the checked-in package inventory policy and manifests to
determine which dependencies and artifacts belong in Desktop.

A packaged smoke harness can use an owned HTTP stub to exercise the Workbench
window. It does not prove a connected server session, authentication, device
grants, or live Computer Use acceptance. Keep these evidence boundaries explicit.

Use named development instances and isolated Desktop profiles; see the
[Desktop README](../../apps/desktop/README.md). Packaging, signing, and release
procedures live in [PRODUCTION.md](../../apps/desktop/PRODUCTION.md),
[SIGNING.md](../../apps/desktop/SIGNING.md), and [RELEASE.md](../../RELEASE.md).
